import{_ as t,o,c as r,a0 as a}from"./chunks/framework.D4fUSYzX.js";const u=JSON.parse('{"title":"ADR 0141: The plane resolves its Agent host: herdr, or herdr-core","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"adr/0141-the-plane-resolves-its-agent-host-herdr-or-herdr-core.md","filePath":"adr/0141-the-plane-resolves-its-agent-host-herdr-or-herdr-core.md"}'),s={name:"adr/0141-the-plane-resolves-its-agent-host-herdr-or-herdr-core.md"};function n(d,e,h,c,i,l){return o(),r("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[a('<h1 id="adr-0141-the-plane-resolves-its-agent-host-herdr-or-herdr-core" tabindex="-1">ADR 0141: The plane resolves its Agent host: herdr, or herdr-core <a class="header-anchor" href="#adr-0141-the-plane-resolves-its-agent-host-herdr-or-herdr-core" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;ADR 0141: The plane resolves its Agent host: herdr, or herdr-core&quot;">​</a></h1><p>Status: accepted Date: 2026-11-12</p><p>Records that the plane drives two <strong>Agent hosts</strong> and names them <code>herdr</code> and <code>herdr-core</code>, that one start resolves its host from the operator&#39;s config, then the environment mark, then the default, that the run states the host it resolved beside the fact that resolved it, that both command dialects and the host&#39;s state words live in one module the surfaces may not bypass, and that <a href="./0002-handoffs-run-through-herdr.html">ADR 0002</a>&#39;s &quot;herdr is the only host for agent processes&quot; narrows to &quot;herdr is one of the two hosts&quot;. It moves no control, decides no gate, and lands no code: this record and its three siblings (<a href="./0142-one-run-holds-agents-on-both-hosts-and-the-host-stands-on-the-handoff.html">ADR 0142</a>, <a href="./0143-on-the-herdr-core-host-the-plane-owns-the-worktree-lifecycle.html">ADR 0143</a>, <a href="./0144-on-the-herdr-core-host-the-plane-derives-the-agent-session-record-path.html">ADR 0144</a>) land before the change they govern, the way ADR 0122, ADR 0128, ADR 0129, ADR 0130, ADR 0131, and ADR 0132 did.</p><h2 id="context" tabindex="-1">Context <a class="header-anchor" href="#context" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Context&quot;">​</a></h2><p>ADR 0002 fixed herdr as the only host for Agent processes, and the plane has held to it: at head <code>2d5c3345</code> the sources spell <code>&quot;herdr&quot;</code> in <strong>29 call sites</strong> and build <strong>16 command shapes</strong> over it - <code>agent list</code>, <code>agent start</code>, <code>agent prompt</code>, <code>agent wait</code>, <code>agent read</code>, <code>agent focus</code>, <code>workspace list</code>, <code>workspace get</code>, <code>workspace create</code>, <code>workspace close</code>, <code>tab create</code>, <code>tab list</code>, <code>tab close</code>, <code>pane list</code>, <code>pane close</code>, <code>pane report-agent</code>, <code>pane release-agent</code>, and the three <code>worktree</code> shapes - and read herdr&#39;s refusal codes (<code>agent_name_taken</code>, <code>agent_pane_busy</code>, <code>workspace_not_found</code>, <code>tab_not_found</code>, <code>worktree_not_found</code>) out of those answers.</p><p>herdr-core (<code>hc</code>) is a different program, and it is already on the operator&#39;s machine beside herdr: <code>herdr</code> 0.9.5 with its own server and socket, and <code>herdr-core</code> 0.1.20 running as <code>herdr-core serve</code>. The Herdr desktop app is one of herdr-core&#39;s clients: its own generated instructions state that every terminal and Agent in the desktop window is a herdr-core terminal, and that a terminal carrying the labels <code>herdr.space=&lt;id&gt;</code> and <code>herdr.tab=&lt;id&gt;</code> shows up in a space even when the plane or the operator started it (<code>hc run --keep --label herdr.space=&lt;id&gt; --label herdr.tab=&lt;id&gt; -- claude</code>).</p><p>Measured by hand on 2026-11-12 against <code>hc</code> 0.1.20, in a scratch <code>HC_SESSION</code> with <code>HC_SOCKET</code> and <code>XDG_RUNTIME_DIR</code> under <code>/tmp</code> (the method and every command stand in <a href="https://github.com/SeriousJul/my-little-software-factory/blob/main/docs/verification/agent-host-herdr-core.md" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">the verification record</a>; every probe terminal and the probe core were removed afterwards, and the operator&#39;s own core was never touched):</p><ul><li>herdr-core answers the plane&#39;s whole loop: <code>run</code> with a name, a cwd, and labels; <code>send</code> for a prompt; <code>ls</code> and <code>get</code> for the roster and one record; <code>wait</code> for a turn; <code>read</code> for the screen; <code>kill</code> and <code>rm</code> for cleanup. A record carries <code>name</code>, <code>labels</code>, <code>state: live|finished</code>, <code>status: idle|working|done|blocked|error</code>, <code>turn</code>, <code>exit_code</code>, <code>reason</code>, <code>parent</code>, and <code>argv</code>, and a taken name is refused with the code <code>name_taken</code>.</li><li>herdr-core&#39;s name rule is <code>[a-z][a-z0-9_-]{0,31}</code>. The plane&#39;s own rule in <code>src/naming.ts</code> (<code>HERDR_NAME_MAX_LENGTH = 32</code>, the slug and identity tag) already builds that shape, so the naming module does not move.</li><li>herdr-core knows no workspace, no tab, no pane, and no worktree. Its unit is one terminal, addressed by <code>t&lt;n&gt;</code> or by name, carrying labels.</li><li><code>hc ls --json</code> carries <strong>no</strong> status word; the status lives in <code>hc get &lt;terminal&gt; --json</code>. The roster alone cannot run the observation cycle.</li><li>On Unix every herdr-core command starts its session&#39;s core when none runs: <code>hc server status</code> on a fresh session answered with a core whose <code>uptime_ms</code> was <code>8</code>. There is no read that answers without starting one.</li><li>A start from a non-terminal gets a <code>200x120</code> screen, and the child program receives <code>HC_TERMINAL_ID</code>.</li><li>A finished terminal keeps its address, its <code>reason: &quot;exited&quot;</code>, and its <code>exit_code</code> until <code>hc rm</code> removes it, so a started-then-dead Agent stays a readable fact.</li><li>The status words are the same five herdr reports, because both read the program&#39;s OSC 7501 reports, and herdr-core&#39;s own manual says the rule the plane already follows: &quot;Do not guess status from screen text.&quot;</li></ul><p>Two of the plane&#39;s facts have no herdr-core answer at all: the Goto&#39;s view focus, and the agent session-record path the Turn log reads (ADR 0008). Those are the reason this decision names its limits instead of claiming parity.</p><h2 id="decision" tabindex="-1">Decision <a class="header-anchor" href="#decision" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Decision&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>The concept is the Agent host, and there are two of them.</strong> The Agent host is the program that runs an Agent&#39;s terminal and holds that Agent&#39;s facts while it runs. <code>herdr</code> and <code>herdr-core</code> are its only values, and no surface invents a third word for the same thing: the glossary&#39;s <strong>Agent host</strong> entry carries the <code>_Avoid_</code> list. ADR 0002&#39;s rule that the plane never starts an Agent process itself stands unchanged; its rule that herdr is the only host does not.</p><p><strong>Host resolution: config, then the environment mark, then the default.</strong> An explicit <code>agent-host</code> in the Config file wins. With no config, a non-empty <code>HERDR_ENV</code> names <code>herdr</code> - the same in-herdr mark ADR 0024 and ADR 0139 already read, set with any value - and a non-empty <code>HC_TERMINAL_ID</code> names <code>herdr-core</code>. With neither mark the answer is <code>herdr-core</code>. Absent is the only word for &quot;resolve it yourself&quot;: no <code>&quot;auto&quot;</code> value stands beside it, and a value outside the two names is a boot refusal through the same strict config rule that refuses an unknown key today.</p><p><strong>The run states what it resolved, and it starts no host of its own.</strong> One Message line and one Plane boot record entry (ADR 0129) name the resolved host and the fact that resolved it: the config key, the herdr mark, the herdr-core mark, or the default. The same line states that the first herdr-core read starts that session&#39;s core when none runs, because the measurement shows no herdr-core command that answers without starting it. The plane runs no <code>server start</code>, <code>server stop</code>, or <code>server restart</code> of either host, and it pre-checks nothing it cannot check without causing the effect.</p><p><strong>The config carries the host and the host&#39;s own connection.</strong> Top-level <code>agent-host = &quot;herdr&quot; | &quot;herdr-core&quot;</code>, and <code>[agent-host.herdr-core]</code> with <code>binary</code>, <code>session</code>, <code>cols</code>, and <code>rows</code>. An unset <code>session</code> is herdr-core&#39;s own default session, the one the operator&#39;s terminals and the Herdr desktop use, and the plane scopes its own Agents by label rather than by taking a private session the operator cannot see into.</p><p><strong>One module owns both command dialects and the host&#39;s state words.</strong> Every herdr and herdr-core command the plane builds leaves through it, and the host words (<code>state</code>, <code>status</code>, <code>turn</code>, <code>reason</code>, and their refusal codes) become the plane&#39;s facts - Starting, running, settle cause, Missing agent - inside it, so no surface reads a host word of its own. An automated check rejects a literal <code>&quot;herdr&quot;</code> or <code>&quot;hc&quot;</code> argument outside that module, the way the shared-controls check rejects a renderer field name today. The <strong>Stub herdr world</strong> (ADR 0132) answers both dialects from one standing, and its closed-surface rule covers both: a suite that starts an Agent on one host cannot be answered by the other host&#39;s shape by accident.</p><p><strong>Every herdr-core start carries its flags, and the plane writes no herdr-core config.</strong> <code>--outlive-parent</code>, because a herdr-core terminal is a child of the terminal that started it and ends with it otherwise, and the plane restarts while Agents run. <code>--no-notify</code>, because herdr-core types notes about a child into the parent&#39;s input, and the plane&#39;s Action bar owns its terminal&#39;s input. No <code>--keep</code>, because a lost Agent replaced by a shell in place hides exactly the fact the Missing agent marker exists to state. No <code>--rm</code>, because the finished record is what the residue record and the operator&#39;s Close act on.</p><p><strong>The plane labels every herdr-core terminal it starts, and adopts it into the desktop when its own terminal stands there.</strong> Its own namespace carries the run, the Ticket, the Handoff, and the repository, which is what scopes the roster read and keeps a dead run&#39;s rows readable. Beside those it copies the Adoption labels <code>herdr.space</code> and <code>herdr.tab</code> from its own herdr-core terminal when that terminal carries them, and writes no such label when it does not. This is what keeps an Agent started from a desktop pane visible in the desktop window at all: on this host the plane has no view to aim, and the operator&#39;s only other route to an Agent is to type an attach command.</p><p><strong>The version floor is the release the shapes were read against: herdr-core 0.1.20.</strong> An older host is one Warning line at boot, and a shape that host refuses comes back as that host&#39;s own reason. The plane does not guess which shape an older core refuses.</p><p><strong>Two herdr facts stay herdr-only, stated rather than faked.</strong> The Goto on this host refuses and states the exact <code>hc attach &lt;name&gt;</code> command the operator types, the way a refusal elsewhere in the plane hands the operator the next step. The Live view&#39;s ANSI stream is not offered: herdr-core reads a screen as text and answers no styled read, and a Live view built from a fake of that stream is worse than a Live view that is absent.</p><h2 id="considered-options" tabindex="-1">Considered options <a class="header-anchor" href="#considered-options" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Considered options&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li><strong>Drive herdr-core everywhere, and let the Adoption labels carry the desktop.</strong> Rejected for now, and it is the direction this decision keeps the door open for: the plane&#39;s stored Environment facts are a workspace, a tab, and a pane, and herdr-core answers none of them, so this change adds a host beside the one the plane ships against instead of replacing it.</li><li><strong>No host at all when neither mark stands: the plane runs read-only.</strong> Rejected: a plain terminal is a first-class way to run the plane, and herdr-core answers there with nothing installed beyond the binary. The run states the resolved host rather than refusing to start anything.</li><li><strong>herdr as the fallback default.</strong> Rejected on the measurement: in a plain terminal no herdr server answers, and the plane&#39;s first handoff fails on a command that cannot reach one.</li><li><strong>Pin the host once per run.</strong> Rejected in <a href="./0142-one-run-holds-agents-on-both-hosts-and-the-host-stands-on-the-handoff.html">ADR 0142</a>: the operator asked to choose at launch, and a Ticket that moves host mid-cycle must not be forced onto one host for its whole life.</li><li><strong>Set the flags in the operator&#39;s <code>~/.config/herdr-core/config.toml</code>.</strong> Rejected: that file is the operator&#39;s and covers terminals the plane does not own, and one flag there (<code>notes</code>, <code>shell</code>, <code>draft_wait</code>) changes the operator&#39;s own terminals to serve the plane&#39;s starts.</li><li><strong>Refuse to run until the operator names a host.</strong> Rejected: the mark is a fact the machine already states, and a refusal that ignores it spends the operator&#39;s attention on a question the environment answers.</li></ul><h2 id="consequences" tabindex="-1">Consequences <a class="header-anchor" href="#consequences" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Consequences&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li>The plane&#39;s runtime dependency is now &quot;one Agent host&quot;, not herdr. Inside herdr the default is herdr and nothing changes for today&#39;s operator; outside herdr the plane drives a host it has never driven, and that is where the risk of this change sits.</li><li>The plane&#39;s state begins to hold a herdr-core terminal address beside a pane, tab, or workspace id, and every reader of those handles reads the host with them (<a href="./0142-one-run-holds-agents-on-both-hosts-and-the-host-stands-on-the-handoff.html">ADR 0142</a>).</li><li>Two command dialects and one host word map have one home, and the check keeps the spelling out of the surfaces. The Stub herdr world grows a second dialect, and its closed-surface rule is what keeps the two from drifting.</li><li>The plane writes into another program&#39;s label namespace (<code>herdr.space</code>, <code>herdr.tab</code>), on the condition that its own terminal carries them. A desktop that changes that rule is a measured break, not a silent one, and the adoption walk is recorded as unverified until it runs.</li><li>The Worktree Environment and the session-record path become the plane&#39;s own on this host, which is two new contracts with git and with the runtime&#39;s on-disk layout rather than a reuse of herdr&#39;s (<a href="./0143-on-the-herdr-core-host-the-plane-owns-the-worktree-lifecycle.html">ADR 0143</a>, <a href="./0144-on-the-herdr-core-host-the-plane-derives-the-agent-session-record-path.html">ADR 0144</a>).</li><li>herdr-core&#39;s own status words map onto the plane&#39;s facts through one table, so a host that renames a word is one module&#39;s change and not a surface&#39;s.</li><li>The Goto and the ANSI Live view read as host-dependent availability facts with a stated reason, and no control appears in the Key guide that this host cannot run.</li><li>Screen-reader support remains unverified, as before: this decision moves no accessibility surface, and the terminal walks in <a href="https://github.com/SeriousJul/my-little-software-factory/blob/main/docs/verification/shared-controls.md" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">the shared-controls verification record</a> stay open. Every herdr-core answer shape the plane reads is traced to a recorded herdr-core answer or recorded as unverified, the ADR 0132 rule, and nothing about this host has been run through the plane yet.</li></ul>',23)])])}const g=t(s,[["render",n]]);export{u as __pageData,g as default};
